👉🏻Twisted Pair Cable
-----Definition
A twisted pair cable is a cable made up of two insulated copper wires twisted together to reduce interference and noise.
-----What is it?
It is a common type of wired transmission medium used to transmit data through electrical signals.
-----How it works
➡️Data is converted into electrical signals.
➡️The signals travel through the copper wires.
➡️The wires are twisted to reduce electromagnetic interference.
➡️The receiver receives and converts the signals back into data.
-----Features
➡️Made of copper wires.
➡️Two wires are twisted together.
➡️Lightweight and flexible.
➡️Easy to install.
➡️Available as UTP and STP.
➡️Commonly used in telephone and computer networks.
-----Advantages
➡️Low cost.
➡️Easy to install.
➡️Flexible and lightweight.
➡️Easily available.
➡️Suitable for short-distance communication.
----Disadvantages
➡️Limited transmission distance.
➡️More affected by noise and interference.
➡️Lower bandwidth than optical fibre.
➡️Signals become weaker over long distances.
👉🏻Coaxial Cable
-----Definition
A coaxial cable is a cable consisting of a central copper conductor surrounded by insulation, a metallic shield, and an outer protective covering.
-----What is it?
It is a wired transmission medium used to carry electrical signals for communication.
-----How it works
➡️Data is converted into electrical signals.
➡️The signals travel through the central copper conductor.
➡️The metallic shield protects the signal from external interference.
➡️The receiver receives the signal and converts it into usable data.
------Features
➡️Has a central copper conductor.
➡️Has an insulating layer.
➡️Has a metallic shield.
➡️Has an outer protective covering.
➡️Better protection from noise than twisted pair.
➡️Used for TV, cable internet and networking.
-----Advantages
➡️Higher bandwidth than twisted pair.
➡️Less affected by noise.
➡️More reliable than twisted pair.
➡️Can transmit signals over longer distances.
➡️Strong and durable.
-----Disadvantages
➡️More expensive than twisted pair.
➡️Thicker and less flexible.
➡️Installation is more difficult.
➡️Lower speed and capacity than optical fibre.
👉🏻Optical Fibre
-----Definition
Optical fibre is a thin, flexible cable made of glass or plastic that transmits data in the form of light signals.
-----What is it?
It is a high-speed transmission medium that uses light instead of electrical signals to transfer data.
-----How it works
➡️Data is converted into light signals.
➡️The light travels through the fibre by total internal reflection.
➡️The light reaches the receiving end.
➡️The receiver converts the light signals back into electrical signals/data.
-----Features
➡️Made of glass or plastic.
➡️Uses light signals.
➡️Very high bandwidth.
➡️Very high transmission speed.
➡️Lightweight and thin.
➡️Not affected by electromagnetic interference.
➡️Suitable for long-distance communication.
-----Advantages
➡️Very high speed.
➡️Very high bandwidth.
➡️Can transmit data over long distances.
➡️Not affected by electromagnetic interference.
➡️More secure because it is difficult to tap.
➡️Lightweight and small in size.
-----Disadvantages
➡️More expensive.
➡️Installation is difficult.
➡️Requires special equipment.
➡️Fibre can be damaged if bent excessively.
➡️Maintenance and repair can be difficult.